a group with no `chord`, and every shipped group has one. A rail button's menu, and a rail page's row of the Pages view, reset that page to its arrangement, and Settings resets every rail page, removes the pages of buttons the rail no longer has and puts the floats of `config/layout.yml` back in place of the floats held (`resetPlan`, `resetAsk` and `resetAfterAsking` of `state/workbench/navigation/pages.ts`, `resetPage` and `resetPages` of the layout store); each asks first through a confirmation naming the pages, then asks each tab of those pages holding unsaved text the question closing that tab asks, one at a time, and a no to any ends the reset with nothing changed; a page the reader made is never touched. Every write of the saved layout is also recorded: the page gives each browser tab an id for its life under `oculus.layout.tab` in `sessionStorage`, and each write appends one entry to a list of the last 500 under `oculus.layout.writes.v1` in `localStorage` (`WRITES_KEY` and `WRITES_KEPT` of `state/workbench/layout/saved/storage.ts`, newest last). An entry is `{ at, tab, call, page, tabs }`: `at` the time in milliseconds, `tab` the browser tab's id, `call` the name of the layout store operation the caller invoked (`openPage`, `closeInstance`), `page` the id of the page on screen, and `tabs` every tab of every page and float written as `<tab id>:<view id>`, so a tab that is missing from one entry and present in the one before shows which browser tab wrote the entry it went missing in. A write equal to the newest entry in all but its time, such as each move of a sash drag, is counted on that entry as `n` (the writes it stands for) and `last` (the time of the latest), so a drag keeps the older entries. The record reads and writes only those two keys, and a full, refused or unparsable storage loses the record and never the layout write.
 - Floats are saved beside the pages: the envelope is `{ version: 6, pages, current, floats }`, each float `{ id, pane, place }`; a version 4 or 3 envelope is read with no floats, and a browser holding one takes the floats of `config/layout.yml` once (the shipped floats reach the page with the configuration, after the saved layout is read, so a layout read from a version 4 envelope is written back at version 4 by `serialiseFloatless` until the shipped floats have been taken in, `needsFloats` and the store's `pendingFloats` marking it, and at version 6 from then on; a float-less layout written at version 5 or 6 would read as a reader who has no floats and the shipped ones would never arrive), each tab of a view a shipped float holds moving out of its page into that float with its instance id and state kept (`adoptFloats`); a float that does not add up (no tab, a place past the window, an id a page or another float already uses, a tab id held twice, a second float docked on one edge) is dropped on reading and the rest kept. `config/layout.yml` carries the top-level `floats:` list, each entry `{ view, dock, size }` or `{ view, x, y, w, h }` with `view` as an arrangement pane's `view`, read by `parseFloats` of `state/workbench/navigation/arrangement.ts`; a browser with no saved layout starts with them and Reset all pages gives them back. The first entry docks Live HPC and Usage at the right edge, so the Focus page is the Focus board alone. The border is the token `--float-border`, `--c-orange` by default and so each theme's own orange, declared on `body` in `themes/tokens.css`. A float's menus offer Dock Left, Dock Right, Dock Bottom (each left out for the edge the float is on), Float on a docked float and Return to Page, and no split, fold or maximise; a tab's state is held under its instance id, so a float can later move to a window of its own. A pane has an optional `locked` flag (Lock Group in the strip menu, `setLocked` of `layout/tree.ts`): a locked pane is skipped when a document opens (`landingFor`), still takes a tab dragged onto it, keeps its lock through activate, open, move, split and close, is saved as `locked: true` inside the envelope (a pane without the field is read as unlocked), and keeps its place when its last tab closes, so only Close pane removes it. A tab is pinned with `pinned: true` (`pinTab` and `unpinTab` of `layout/open/pinned.ts`): a pane keeps its pinned tabs first, in their order, then the rest, and a saved pane whose pinned tabs are not first is put in that order on reading; a pinned tab is never a preview (the two flags together read as pinned), keeps its pin through a float, a move to another page and a split, and lands in the pinned run of the pane it goes to, a drag into the run pins it and a drag out of it unpins it. A pane holds `recent`, the ids of the other tabs it showed, the one shown last first, and closing the active tab shows the first of them still open, else the tab that took its place; `recent` is saved with the pane, so a version 5 envelope, which holds none, reads with the active tab as the only one used. A new tab opens after the active tab (`openedAt` of `layout/tree.ts`), after the pinned run when the active tab is pinned, which is VS Code's `openPositioning: right`; the preview tab's replacement, that position and the unlimited tab count are VS Code's defaults and have no setting. The pane of a float is lockable the same way: its strip menu offers Lock Group and Unlock Group, and the lock stays through a dock, a move, a return to a page and a save and read back. A saved page tied to the rail key `info` is folded into the page tied to `views` when a layout is read (a views page holding no system view gets the info page's system views in a new pane below the pane holding Pages, split in halves, and one still labelled Views and Settings takes the label System; the info page's own splits, sizes and label are not carried), and a saved `config` tab becomes a `settings` tab with its state dropped (`state/workbench/layout/saved/migrate.ts` and `fold.ts`), so a browser that saved the two old system pages opens the one System page with its tabs.
 - Opened views land beside the tools. A view registered with no catalogue group is opened for something (a project, a person, a deposit, a manifest edit, a hub document, a file, a source file, a diff, a merge, a note, a customization file), and it lands, in order, in the pane that received the page's newest such tab while that tab is there, the reader's pane when it already holds such tabs, an empty documents pane, an empty terminals pane, a pane holding such tabs alone, or a new pane split to the right of the reader's (`landingFor` of `state/workbench/layout/open/landing.ts`). A pane holding a tool view takes one only when the reader is in it and it already holds opened tabs, so a document opened from a card never covers the board the card is on. A tool view that asks for the tab (`openDiff` for the Search view's Replace Preview, which passes the asking instance to `openOpened`) names its own pane as `away` of `landingFor`: that pane is taken out of every step, and when no other pane takes the tab the new pane is split to the right of it, so the tool view stays on screen. The tabs a page received are held per page by the attention store and not saved, so after a reload the pane of documents alone takes their place. An arrangement may name an empty pane (`view: []`), which draws one line saying opened files land there; the Explorer page carries one, and a pane kept empty when its last opened tab went is the same pane. An empty terminals pane says terminals started from its menu appear there. The rule reads the registry through `registerOpened`, called once by the client context, so the layout store never imports the registry.
 - The public deck is a live oculus view and never a generated file. `present` is a listed view of the Present group drawing one slide at a time, with the arrow keys, the on-screen controls and `O` for an overview grid; the six kinds are components under `views/academic/present/parts/`. The deck's own slides, the domain themes and the order live in `config/present.yml` as the `deck:` key, read by the config loader and pushed like every other config file; a project's slides are the `present:` front matter of its PRESENT.md, read into `Project.present` and carried in the snapshot, with a `slides:` list beside it for further slides of the same project under `<project>/<slug>` ids. LaTeX is typeset in the page by KaTeX, the one new dependency, and a figure is served through the file route. A slide is drawn in a 16:9 frame of 1280 by 720 px, scaled uniformly to its pane and centred in it, so the deck fills the pane at any size; the frame draws its own surface, carries the slide type scale in px at that size, and scrolls a slide that is taller than it. The overview grid and the empty state are not scaled.
-- A new session starts from the composer in a Terminal tab: the workspace (with its branch shown read-only from the git channel), agent, account and the pickers `config/catalogs/agents.yml` lists, parsed by `data/sources/config/catalog/agents.ts` with every refused line a finding. The start runs through tmux as an argument vector of at least two words (`env` in front of a one-word command), with the account's folder as one `-e` pair (or `env -u` for the default account) and the prompt as one argv word after `--`, so no shell reads either; a shell start hides the pickers and types its prompt as its first command. A running session is steered with its text as bytes through `send-keys -H` and Enter, and Stop sends Escape; a session running outside oculus shows the steer box read-only with a sentence, and New brings the composer. The steer box is drawn under a Claude or Codex terminal and never under a shell. Each session view carries a session picker, except the Terminal view. A Terminal tab is a terminal panel of its own: it owns one or more terminals, a terminal belongs to exactly one tab, and the tab's layout state holds them in the tab's own order with the row drawn side by side, each terminal's share of the row's width, the focused one and whether the composer shows (`state/sessions/terminal/terminal-tabs.ts`, which reads an older saved tab into that shape, a pinned tab keeping its terminal and any other showing the composer). The tab draws, from two terminals on, the list of its own terminals on the right of its body, each row titled after what runs in it (a task terminal after the project command it ran), and one row while it shows the composer with one terminal owned, and with its only terminal shown names it on its bar. New in a tab starts a shell at once, in the folder of the terminal shown in it (else the tab's first terminal's, else the hub root, waited for while the portfolio has not arrived), and the bar's dropdown beside it starts a Shell, Claude or Codex the same way (New Terminal With Profile); With a Prompt shows the composer right of the row for the first message, in the tab's folder. The pane menu's New Terminal starts a shell the same way in a Terminal tab of the pane it was picked on (`openInPane` of the layout), while the `n` key and the Sessions view's New keep the composer, in the tab's folder. A refused start is said on the bar and leaves the tab as it was. Split starts a terminal of the split terminal's kind in its folder at once and puts it right of that terminal; the store keeps the answered start until the sessions payload lists it. A row holds any number of terminals (`row`, `shares` and `shown`), each at least 80 px wide (`rowWidths`), the body scrolling sideways when they do not fit, and a handle between two terminals moves their boundary. Unsplit takes a terminal out of a row of more than one, Join adds a terminal the tab owns to the row, and a press on a terminal outside the row shows it alone. Rename (the menus, or a double-click on a row's name) sets the tmux user option `@oculus_title`, Change Icon and Change Color set `@oculus_icon` and `@oculus_color` (the strip tab of a Terminal tab is drawn with the shown terminal's icon and colour, `shell/panes/pane-view.ts` `stripMark`), and Clear empties the grid, the tmux screen (`send-keys -R`) and the tmux history (`clear-history`), all through `server/machine/pty/edit.ts`; Copy as HTML writes the selection as `text/html` and `text/plain` through `@xterm/addon-serialize`. A row dragged within the list reorders the tab, and one dropped on another Terminal tab's list, or on the body of one drawing no list, moves there, a tab left owning nothing closing unless it shows the composer (`moveTerminal`). A terminal started anywhere (the update tray, resume and fork, a dataset recovery, a project chat, a LaTeX build, a Repositories shell) is shown by `showTerminal` of `state/sessions/terminals.svelte.ts`: in the tab that owns it, on its page, or, owned by n